WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.280
Ah, subthemes. They can make your deck better, or&nbsp;
they can make your deck worse, and either of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.640
two might be a desirable outcome depending&nbsp;
on what you look for in a game of Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.520
the Gathering. Hi there, I’m Alex, and today&nbsp;
I’m here to tell you about a passion of mine:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.280
making decks do more things. Most EDH decks will&nbsp;
have a theme—Merfolk, tokens, on-attack triggers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.920
and so forth—and this is a nice place to start&nbsp;
in getting a deck to do something. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.440
eventually that one theme might start to&nbsp;
get a little boring, and your eyes might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.000
begin to wander. That on-attack trigger card&nbsp;
makes tokens, can I play harder into that? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.840
really like this zombie creature that makes me&nbsp;
discard cards, can I make my deck focus more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.480
discard? This ETB creature searches up legendary&nbsp;
cards, I wonder how that might fit into my blink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.320
deck. And this makes sense! Having your deck&nbsp;
do more stuff, have more textured play paths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.960
produce more unexpected results—all of&nbsp;
this will be desirable to a lot of players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.520
particularly players who have already played a&nbsp;
lot with more linear styles of deck. So here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.280
I’m going to be making a little guide on how to&nbsp;
answer the sorts of questions I mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.440
about specific cards you’re finding yourself&nbsp;
transfixed by, and at the end you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.480
an exciting arsenal of brand new questions to&nbsp;
probe your deck with. With that said, let’s begin.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.400
Assuming you’re not trying to obliterate your&nbsp;
deck’s consistency, a good place to start in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.040
expanding your deck’s synergy universe is&nbsp;
going to be what I’ll call fulcrum cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.240
This is a thing that naturally&nbsp;
ties together multiple subthemes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.240
and it can take a variety of different forms,&nbsp;
but typically comes in the form of a payoff from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.880
one theme that rewards another. Throne of Geth&nbsp;
ties together artifact sacrifice and counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.760
Displacer Kitten ties together the&nbsp;
flicker and spellslinger archetypes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.280
and Strength from the Fallen ties together&nbsp;
enchantments and creature-stuffed graveyards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.360
with a resulting payoff that can work well&nbsp;
for a power-matters deck. These are pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.920
clear examples of fulcrum cards, but there are a&nbsp;
lot of other examples that are less obvious but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.240
absolutely there if you go looking. Professional&nbsp;
Facebreaker rewards a board of evasive creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.480
with some artifact tokens that can crack for mana,&nbsp;
Quicksmith Genius rewards artifacts entering with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.000
rummages that can feed graveyard strategies, and&nbsp;
Squee Dubious Monarch rewards a stocked graveyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.720
with a reanimated attacker that generates tokens.&nbsp;
Why were all of those examples red three drops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.160
you might ask? No particular reason, but you&nbsp;
should definitely put them all in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.800
deck. Regardless, all of these cards represent&nbsp;
points of fusion between different archetypes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.040
connective glue that involves setup for one&nbsp;
archetype turning into payoff for another.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.520
An important consideration when thinking about&nbsp;
this sort of card is the floor of the effect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.000
I’m stat-brained so I’d be thinking about&nbsp;
maybe the 25th percentile game for a card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.360
but basically what we’re wondering is how the card&nbsp;
will feel in a subpar situation, where it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.680
living up to our wildest dreams. For some of the&nbsp;
cards I mentioned earlier, the floor is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.160
high. Professional Facebreaker will usually manage&nbsp;
to generate at least a couple treasures, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.280
Displacer Kitten can at least serve as protection&nbsp;
if no juicy ETB creatures are present. For others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.760
the floor might be a lot lower, though. Dross&nbsp;
Scorpion is a card I run in one of my decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.480
that works as a fulcrum point between artifact&nbsp;
creatures hitting graveyard and tap effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.600
but this card could easily end up being a poorly&nbsp;
statted creature with a single untap on death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.480
and that’s important to keep in mind. It gets&nbsp;
even worse for noncreature spells—in my most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.880
recent deck review, one of my supporters was&nbsp;
trying to integrate a small-ish Astral Slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.640
flicker package into a deck, and unfortunately&nbsp;
without any cycling cards in hand that card is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.920
just a three mana textless enchantment. This&nbsp;
is where a bit of discernment is necessary—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.120
important to figure out what works as a minor part&nbsp;
of a deck, and what has no business being outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.760
a deck that doesn’t heavily support it, due to&nbsp;
its low floor. As a related example of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:33.200
I’ve had a handful of people suggest Rhet-Tomb&nbsp;
Mystic for my Astral Slide deck. At first glance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.200
this might make sense—I’m playing a deck trying&nbsp;
to cycle cards, this card gives all my creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.560
cycling, and it’s a Wizard to top it all off,&nbsp;
grabbable with my Wizardcycling cards. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.920
the issue with adding a card like this is that—at&nbsp;
its floor—it will always consume a card slot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.920
could have gone to an ETB creature or some&nbsp;
card draw or a card that itself has cycling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.600
and then how high do you think the ceiling&nbsp;
will be in a deck where over 40% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.920
creatures already have cycling? It looks&nbsp;
good at first, and I’m sure it’s great in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.080
a more general graveyard deck, but once we&nbsp;
do the floor/ceiling calculation it becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.480
clear that this little bird gets mogged by&nbsp;
a Merfolk Looter in this particular deck.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.480
As you might have guessed, a major factor in the&nbsp;
floor/ceiling discussion is the amount of deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.120
space required to support a particular card.&nbsp;
A lot of possible fulcrum cards might already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.360
be within your synergy family of choice, and&nbsp;
might create resources that are already useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.520
to what you’re doing even if they theoretically&nbsp;
might tie naturally into a different subtheme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.920
We can see this with the treasure example earlier,&nbsp;
where treasures are just a naturally useful tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.120
for basically all decks. This stands in contrast&nbsp;
to something like a Doorkeeper in an Arcades the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.240
Strategist deck—how do you make target player&nbsp;
milling feel useful for a theme that usually wins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.720
through combat damage and doesn’t traditionally&nbsp;
utilize the graveyard very much? I’m sure plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.880
of people have done it, but it requires more&nbsp;
divergence from the traditional Arcades gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.440
than a lot of other defenders payoffs, and will&nbsp;
likely demand more deck space to be any good as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.920
a result. Another angle for thinking about deck&nbsp;
space could be to consider how closely interlinked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.240
two different themes are to one another. Sometimes&nbsp;
there will be a whole bunch of fulcrum cards for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.200
a particular set of two themes, with one I’ve&nbsp;
utilized in the past being artifacts and ETB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.280
effects. Thought Monitor, Conjurer’s Closet, Myr&nbsp;
Battlesphere, Mimic Vat, the Mages—there’s a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.880
lot of options there, and if you’re playing a&nbsp;
bunch of these your two-theme deck experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.920
might end up feeling decently compact despite&nbsp;
its somewhat mechanically broad nature. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:29.120
many other sets of two themes have dramatically&nbsp;
less innate shared DNA. If I’m trying to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.240
deck that combines cast-from-exile and&nbsp;
landfall, well there are two landfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.600
cards with exile-you-may-play text, and there are&nbsp;
also cards that reward playing lands from exile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.400
in addition to casting spells which work well&nbsp;
for us, and then if we dive into “from anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.800
other than your hand” effects as well we might&nbsp;
find some roundabout synergies in other landfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.440
cards such as Courser of Kruphix and Hedron&nbsp;
Crab, among others. Hopefully you can see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.640
I’m getting at here—sometimes you’re just gonna&nbsp;
have a bit of a hard time combining two themes.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.400
Now, does this mean that you shouldn’t smash&nbsp;
together two very different synergy families? No!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.680
Artifacts + ETB, Tokens + 1/1 counters—these have&nbsp;
been done tens of thousands of times before for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.320
good reason, but you always have the opportunity&nbsp;
to do something different, something unique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.120
something more specifically suited to who you&nbsp;
are as a player. What you should do, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.080
is know that’s what you’re doing, figure out why&nbsp;
you’re doing that, and then figure out a plan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.560
action based on what your goals are. Let’s say&nbsp;
you’re building an initiative deck with a heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.840
emphasis on legendary permanents in general and&nbsp;
the background enchantments in particular. Cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.000
a bit tricky, but there are some possible points&nbsp;
of connection here. Next, it’s important to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.640
out the reason for building the deck, because&nbsp;
that will affect the decisions you make. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.960
trying to build a decent bracket 3 deck with this&nbsp;
set of restrictions as a challenge, you’ll want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.520
to dial in hard on the points of cohesion, and&nbsp;
consider ways you can get the different elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.840
to come together more often. Maybe we run partner&nbsp;
commanders to get full use out of Dungeon Delver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.000
and pick colors that offer the most utility in&nbsp;
putting together our gameplan. If we decide that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.320
Dungeon Delver is a key card for us, maybe we&nbsp;
add some cards like Enlightened Tutor, Muddle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.400
the Mixture, and a couple other helpful tutors in&nbsp;
whatever colors we end up in so that we can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.960
that card more often, and then maybe we pair that&nbsp;
with other useful cards that fit the same tutoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.280
restrictions. If we have the goal of getting the&nbsp;
most mechanically fluid version of initiative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.000
legendaries, there are some cool decision paths&nbsp;
we can run down, and the end result will likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.040
be a very unique bracket 3 deck. Some people&nbsp;
might worry that tutors will homogenize the play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.920
experience of a deck, but this won’t necessarily&nbsp;
be true—card selection tools like filtering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.640
spells and conditional tutors will reduce the play&nbsp;
pattern variance of a deck, but if you add them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.800
a deck that would otherwise have extreme levels&nbsp;
of play pattern variance due to having a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.920
of moving parts, you’ll likely end up in a place&nbsp;
that is still very fresh and dynamic to play with.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.040
Now let’s say we’re building with a different&nbsp;
goal: we’re just trying to have a good time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.560
and are likely aiming for more of a laid back,&nbsp;
bracket 2 style deck. This is also a very valid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.640
goal, and honestly it’s a pretty fantastic reason&nbsp;
to make a deck that might be just a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.360
mechanically dubious. If your starting point is&nbsp;
a naturally slower-paced political mechanic like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.440
initiative, and if you’re introducing extra&nbsp;
layers of synergy rather than filling your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.520
deck with high quality staple creatures, you’re&nbsp;
going to end up with something that plays quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.200
well at lower power tables. You’ll want to&nbsp;
make sure you’re running enough filtering so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.720
that your deck functions and so that you’re&nbsp;
having fun throughout the whole process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.480
but the play pattern of a deck that gradually&nbsp;
filters for different elements of its cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:28.080
funky gameplan is one that is perfect for a&nbsp;
bracket 2 game in both pace and nature. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.480
a final note here, playing at a lower power table&nbsp;
is also a great place for a somewhat mechanically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.520
disjointed deck because incentives align perfectly&nbsp;
on what sorts of vegetables you should be running,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.320
particularly removal. Lower power opponents&nbsp;
won’t require all the best removal the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.040
has to offer, and this means you can do something&nbsp;
that’s already nice to do: run some of the higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:53.040
mana options with added utility for your synergy&nbsp;
families. My bracket 2 incubators deck has 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.360
cards I’ve categorized as interaction which is&nbsp;
a decent number, but if I click through these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.920
on the Archidekt page literally all of them do&nbsp;
something else for the deck. This is nice for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.720
keeping a mechanically messy deck from feeling too&nbsp;
bad, and it also lets me play with cards that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.360
a bit more unique within the commander format. Is&nbsp;
Spread the Sickness the best removal this game has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:17.920
to offer? Definitely not, but it feels immensely&nbsp;
at home in this deck, and I wouldn’t dare cut it.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.160
Thanks for watching! I’m going to be at MagicCon&nbsp;
Vegas in just a week or so, and I’ll be hosting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.400
a panel along with The Trinket Mage and a&nbsp;
couple friends of the podcast. Come watch that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:31.200
or get a sticker, or just say hi, whatever floats&nbsp;
your boat. If you want to support what I do here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.280
you can grab some high quality sleeves from&nbsp;
dragonshield, affiliate link in description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.200
or you can support me more directly&nbsp;
through Patreon or a YouTube membership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.200
These lovely people, some of whom are onscreen&nbsp;
now, help let me keep doing what I do here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.640
and they also receive bonus videos from&nbsp;
me once per month or so, plus a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.560
at a deck review every three months. If&nbsp;
you want to join this illustrious crowd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.440
the links and/or buttons to do so are&nbsp;
probably on your screen somewhere!

